import "./components.cade" as common
import "./datums.cade" as datum

-- ETA positions 25 to 31: the automatic winding module.
--
-- Re-derived 2026-09-06 on module 0.12, from the STEPPED PROFILES of the
-- elevations on the materials page. Several of these parts are two diameters
-- on one arbor and the bounding boxes hid it; reading width against height
-- recovers both, and the pinion diameters are what settle the module. See
-- `datum.winding_module` for that argument.
--
-- RE-SOLVED 2026-09-06 IN SERIES, from photographs of the module. The chain
-- was previously wired in PARALLEL - the rotor driving both reversing wheels
-- and both driving the reduction wheel - which left the two reversing wheels
-- 8.14 apart with tip radii summing to 6.45. A 1.70 gap. They never touched,
-- and a pair that has to carry the rotor's torque in both directions carried
-- nothing. Every photograph of this module shows them side by side and
-- meshing. See ERRORS.md E10.
--
--   rotor ring 45 (5.61)       -> aux reversing ring A 36 (4.60)  C 4.8600
--   aux reversing ring B 33    -> reversing ring A 36 (4.60)      C 4.1400
--   reversing ring B 33 (4.24) -> reduction ring 40 (4.99)        C 4.3800
--   reduction pinion 8 (1.16)  -> driving ring 47 (5.87)          C 3.3000
--   driving pinion 10 (1.43)   -> ratchet wheel 63 (7.47)         C 4.1939
--
--   1 rotor turn = 0.02554 ratchet turns, so 39.2 turns of the weight wind
--   the ratchet once. It was 34.8 on the parallel wiring.
--
-- The driving pinion now meshes the ratchet wheel on the RATCHET's module,
-- 0.1149, not 0.12 -- the ratchet is 63 teeth since E9 closed, and a pinion
-- meshes the wheel it drives.
--
-- HOW THE POSITIONS WERE FOUND. The five centre distances above are exact:
-- each is m(z1+z2)/2 from the counts, the way the going train and the winding
-- train were solved. That leaves the chain free to fold either way about the
-- rotor, and the photographs choose the fold: the wheels run as one arc
-- hugging the rotor seat, not scattered across the plate. The angular branch
-- is therefore photo-GUIDED rather than photo-measured - every frame of this
-- module is oblique, and none of them has a scale in it. The centre distances
-- are exact; the angles are good to a few degrees and no better.
--
-- The reversing wheels are NOT a wheel and pinion. Their elevations show two
-- toothed rings of nearly the same diameter, 4.60 and 4.24, which is what a
-- reversing wheel with internal pawls looks like: one ring takes the drive
-- from the rotor, the other passes it on, and the pawls between them lock in
-- one direction and slip in the other. Whichever way the weight swings, one
-- of the pair drives and the other free-wheels. The pawls are not modelled.
--
-- Only the driving wheel's pinion reaches the ratchet. A 5.87mm ring dipping
-- to the ratchet's level would cut through both bridges, so the ring stays
-- above the automatic seat and the pinion drops through a relief in the
-- barrel bridge.
--
--
-- THE ROTOR'S RIM IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, and a first solve missed it. The
-- weight carries its mass in a rim whose bore is 8.6, and the whole module runs
-- inside that bore, so every wheel needs |r| + tip <= 8.6. A solve without it
-- put the reversing wheel at |r| 7.72, tip reaching 10.0, straight into the rim
-- - 0.093 of overlap, which the audit found. With the rim in, the four wheels
-- close on an arc at -10.8, +30.1, +71.4 and +103.8 degrees about the rotor
-- axis: a 115 degree span, against the 117 measured off the flat photograph of
-- the module. That agreement was not fitted; the angles fall out of the five
-- exact centre distances once the rim and the balance sweep are respected.
--
-- A NOTE ON THE TEETH. The chain is now a simple series, so every wheel has
-- at most two meshes and each is phased against the one that drives it: a
-- tooth of the driver on the line of centres, a space of the driven facing it.
-- Axial levels. The order is forced: the reversing wheels take the drive from
-- the rotor's ring at the top and hand it to the reduction wheel below, so
-- each of them spans two levels -- which is also why ETA fits them between
-- the driving wheel and the reduction wheel.
--
--   3.35  framework            0.20
--   3.57  driving ring         0.14   with the reduction pinion
--   3.75  reduction ring       0.14   with reversing ring B
--   3.93  rotor ring           0.14   with reversing ring A
--   4.11  lower bridge         0.14
--   4.30  the weight's plate, inside whose rim the whole module runs
-- Thinned 0.14 -> 0.11 and the pitch 0.18 -> 0.13. The old stack needed
-- 0.14 + 0.50 + 0.20 = 0.86 of the 0.75 between the automatic seat at 3.35 and
-- the weight's hub at 4.10, so the top two wheels ended up INSIDE the framework
-- and had to be given 2.4 clearance holes -- which is why they read as sitting
-- on top of the plate instead of under it. At 0.11 on 0.13 the whole chain fits
-- beneath a solid framework, which is what the photographs show: the wheels are
-- seen through narrow crescent openings, not through holes their own size.
fn ring_thickness() -> Len {
  0.11mm
}

fn ring_pitch() -> Len {
  0.13mm
}

-- Dropped 0.06 from +0.29. The three rings sit at this, +0.18 and +0.36; with the
-- framework now the OUTER member at 3.85 to 4.05, the reduction ring's top was at
-- 3.89 and cut 0.249 mm^3 into it. At +0.23 the rings top out at 3.65, 3.83 and
-- 4.01: the lower two pass under the plate, which is what lets it cover them, and
-- only the rotor-level ring comes through -- which is 27's, and has its hole.
-- Still 0.09 clear of the lower bridge at 3.49 underneath.
fn driving_ring_z() -> Len {
  datum.automatic_seat_z() + 0.20mm
}

fn reduction_ring_z() -> Len {
  driving_ring_z() + ring_pitch()
}

fn rotor_ring_z() -> Len {
  reduction_ring_z() + ring_pitch()
}

fn reduction_pinion_drop() -> Len {
  reduction_ring_z() - driving_ring_z()
}

-- The driving wheel's pinion reaches down to the ratchet wheel, through the
-- automatic seat and a relief in the barrel bridge.
fn driving_pinion_z() -> Len {
  datum.ratchet_wheel_seat_z() + datum.ratchet_wheel_thickness() / 2
}

fn driving_pinion_drop() -> Len {
  driving_ring_z() - driving_pinion_z()
}

-- The oscillating weight's toothed ring, cut around the bearing's races.
-- Position 31-2 is one part: a ball bearing with this ring around its outer
-- race. 5.61mm over the teeth on module 0.12 is 45 teeth.
part eta_pos_31_3_rotor_pinion() -> Solid<Exact> {
  common.involute_disk(
    root_r = 2.55mm,
    bore_r = datum.rotor_bearing_seat_radius(),
    flank_nx = 0.28203,
    flank_ny = 0.95941,
    flank_d = 0.83768mm,
    inner_x = 2.295mm,
    tip_r = 2.82mm,
    h = ring_thickness(),
    teeth = 45,
  )
}

-- Positions 27 and 28 are the same manufactured reversing wheel, so they are
-- the same definition and carry the same colour: Cade groups identical
-- definitions by content and refuses two colours on one (GAP-2026-09-05-05).
part eta_pos_27_aux_reversing_wheel_envelope() -> Solid<Exact> {
  -- Ring B below, taking the drive on to the reduction wheel; ring A above,
  -- meshing the rotor. Measured 4.24 and 4.60 over the teeth.
  union(
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 1.83mm,
      bore_r = 0.22mm,
      flank_nx = 0.28378,
      flank_ny = 0.95889,
      flank_d = 0.63688mm,
      inner_x = 1.647mm,
      tip_r = 2.1mm,
      h = ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 33,
    ),
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 2.01mm,
      bore_r = 0.22mm,
      flank_nx = 0.28003,
      flank_ny = 0.95999,
      flank_d = 0.67988mm,
      inner_x = 1.809mm,
      tip_r = 2.28mm,
      h = ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 36,
    )
      |> at(dx = 0mm, dy = 0mm, dz = ring_pitch()),
    cylinder(r = 0.34mm, h = ring_pitch() + ring_thickness())
      |> at(dx = 0mm, dy = 0mm, dz = ring_pitch() / 2),
  )
}

part eta_pos_28_reversing_wheel_envelope() -> Solid<Exact> {
  eta_pos_27_aux_reversing_wheel_envelope()
}

part eta_pos_29_reduction_wheel_envelope(pinion_phase: Angle) -> Solid<Exact> {
  -- Measured 4.99 over the ring and 1.16 over the pinion: 40 teeth and 8
  -- leaves on module 0.12. The pinion hangs below, on the driving wheel.
  union(
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 2.25mm,
      bore_r = 0.22mm,
      flank_nx = 0.27573,
      flank_ny = 0.96123,
      flank_d = 0.7368mm,
      inner_x = 2.025mm,
      tip_r = 2.52mm,
      h = ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 40,
    ),
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 0.33mm,
      bore_r = 0.12mm,
      flank_nx = 0.36933,
      flank_ny = 0.9293,
      flank_d = 0.24939mm,
      inner_x = 0.297mm,
      tip_r = 0.6mm,
      h = reduction_pinion_drop() + ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 8,
    )
      |> rotate(ax = 0, ay = 0, az = 1, angle = pinion_phase)
      |> at(dx = 0mm, dy = 0mm, dz = 0mm - reduction_pinion_drop() / 2),
  )
}

part eta_pos_26_driving_wheel_envelope(pinion_phase: Angle) -> Solid<Exact> {
  -- Measured 5.87 over the ring and 1.43 over the pinion: 47 teeth and 10
  -- leaves on module 0.12. The pinion is what turns the ratchet wheel.
  union(
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 2.67mm,
      bore_r = 0.22mm,
      flank_nx = 0.28548,
      flank_ny = 0.95838,
      flank_d = 0.88179mm,
      inner_x = 2.403mm,
      tip_r = 2.94mm,
      h = ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 47,
    ),
    common.involute_disk(
      root_r = 0.45mm,
      bore_r = 0.15mm,
      flank_nx = 0.35346,
      flank_ny = 0.93545,
      flank_d = 0.28518mm,
      inner_x = 0.405mm,
      tip_r = 0.72mm,
      h = driving_pinion_drop() + ring_thickness(),
      teeth = 10,
    )
      |> rotate(ax = 0, ay = 0, az = 1, angle = pinion_phase)
      |> at(dx = 0mm, dy = 0mm, dz = 0mm - driving_pinion_drop() / 2),
  )
}

-- A pocket for one wheel, sunk from the framework's UNDERSIDE and floored
-- `running_clearance` above the wheel's own top. Local to the framework, whose
-- origin is at mid-thickness.
-- POSITION 25 IS A STACK OF ROUND PLATES, and this is the arithmetic of the
-- stack. Corrected 2026-09-07 from photographs the project lead annotated: the
-- module dismantled with 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30 and 905 labelled on it, and the
-- assembled movement with 25's own outline drawn on the back. The second of
-- those is concentric -- eight circles about the rotor axis -- and
-- `tools/framework_steps.py` reads their radii off it rather than by eye:
--
--     step   r / r_outer   radius mm
--        1       0.1495       1.250     the central post
--        2       0.3633       3.039
--        3       0.4945       4.137
--        4       0.7303       6.109
--        5       0.8032       6.719
--        6       0.8518       7.126     the rim's inner edge
--        7       0.9733       8.142
--        8       1.0000       8.366     the plate's own outer radius
--
-- The ratio column is the measurement; the millimetres are that column against
-- `framework_outline.py`'s 8.366, so steps 7 and 8 landing on the outline this
-- project measured independently is the check rather than the input.
--
-- WHAT THE MODEL HAD. A flat 0.56 slab spanning 3.49 to 4.05, with a blind
-- pocket sunk over each wheel and floored 0.05 above it -- which left a lid of
-- plate over every wheel: MEASURED at 6.228, 2.067, 2.029 and 2.769 mm^3 above
-- 26, 27, 28 and 29, a skin 0.135 thick. **The interference audit passed it.**
-- `intersect(25, wheel)` is 0.000 mm^3 for all four, before and after, because
-- a lid 135 microns thick is not an interference -- it is a wrong part that
-- clears, and no instrument here asks whether a part is the right one. What
-- found it was the picture, where the wheels read as buried in the plate.
--
-- WHAT IT IS INSTEAD. Four bands, and every height is forced by what the band
-- has to clear rather than chosen:
--
--   4.26  the post's top, inside the bearing's bore
--   4.05  the plate's top: `automatic_framework_top_z`, the rotor hub's floor
--         less running clearance
--   3.915 the roof's underside: the wheels top out at 3.865, plus running
--         clearance. Everything from here up may pass over a wheel
--   3.70  the counterbore's floor, where the bearing lands
--   3.49  the rim's underside: it sits on the lower bridge
--   3.35  the automatic seat, where the central foot and the two screw feet land
--
-- and the only radius that is a choice rather than a measurement is the central
-- foot's, which is expert_estimate below.
fn fw_local(z: Len) -> Len {
  z - datum.automatic_framework_seat_z() - datum.automatic_framework_thickness() / 2
}

-- A band of the stack: everything inside `r`, between two WORLD heights, in the
-- part's own local frame.
fn fw_band(r: Len, z0: Len, z1: Len) -> Solid<Exact> {
  cylinder(r = r, h = z1 - z0)
    |> at(dx = 0mm, dy = 0mm, dz = fw_local(z = (z0 + z1) / 2))
}

-- The wheels reach from 1.67 to 7.59 of the rotor axis, so the plate can only
-- go deep inside the first and outside the second. 1.60 is that inner figure
-- less clearance; it is the one radius in this part that is not measured.
-- expert_estimate: bounded above by 26's teeth at 1.67 and below by the post.
fn fw_foot_radius() -> Len {
  1.6mm
}

-- The top of the roof band -- the wheels' own ceiling plus running clearance.
fn fw_roof_floor_z() -> Len {
  rotor_ring_z() + ring_thickness() / 2 + datum.running_clearance()
}

fn wheel_pocket(r: Len, top_z: Len) -> Solid<Exact> {
  let floor_z = top_z + datum.running_clearance()
  let depth = floor_z - datum.automatic_framework_seat_z()
  cylinder(r = r + datum.running_clearance(), h = depth)
    |> at(
      dx = 0mm,
      dy = 0mm,
      dz = 0mm - datum.automatic_framework_thickness() / 2 + depth / 2,
    )
}

part eta_pos_25_automatic_framework_envelope() -> Solid<Exact> {
  -- MEASURED. The outline below is the part's own silhouette, read off page 6
  -- of the technical communication -- which draws every component in plan,
  -- orthographically, and which this project has been calibrating on since the
  -- first session without ever reading the shapes. Four drawn shapes preceded
  -- this one. See ERRORS.md E12.
  --
  -- 13.41 x 16.36 as drawn, against the 20.8 across this part had been given.
  -- It was not only the wrong shape; it was half again too big.
  --
  -- Cade has no polygon, so the silhouette is triangulated and each triangle is
  -- the intersection of its three edge half-spaces with a slab of the part's
  -- thickness. 23 vertices, 21 triangles. Placed by fitting the measured
  -- outline over the pivots it carries and the screws it reaches: all four
  -- pivots sit 1.9 to 3.3 inside the edge, and the two screw datums fall 0.24
  -- outside -- which is a real discrepancy and is recorded, not absorbed.
  -- MEASURED, page 6 blob 92, and now GENERATED: `tools/framework_outline.py`
  -- emits these triangles from the drawing's own contour so the rotation is one
  -- number, not a hand-pasted table. Taken relative to the BEARING BORE, because
  -- that bore is the rotor axis and the plate must be concentric with it. Placing
  -- it by the centroid put the hub 3.7 off the rotor, and the plate stopped
  -- covering the wheels -- which is the entire job of the part.
  -- MEASURED and GENERATED: `tools/framework_outline.py 91.3`.
  --
  -- 91.3 degrees is TRACED, not fitted. The project lead drew this part's outline
  -- in red on a square-on photograph of the movement. That trace fits the page 6
  -- silhouette at IoU 0.94 and 54.00 px/mm, and it puts the part's BEARING BORE
  -- where the balance's own shock setting reads 7.001 from it -- against the
  -- 6.990 of `balance_staff`. An 0.011 agreement on a datum nothing here was
  -- fitted to. Tying the photograph to this frame through that balance gives
  -- 91.3, and an INDEPENDENT sweep -- clear the balance bridge, cover the wheels
  -- -- had already landed on 88. Three supports, two of them measurements.
  --
  -- IT WAS 241.5, AND THAT WAS WRONG. 241.5 came from a bench photograph and was
  -- 'confirmed' by the driving wheel landing 4.183 from the ratchet against the
  -- 4.194 its mesh wants. But that 4.194 is derived from the module-0.12 tooth
  -- counts, and E16 had already shown three of the five chain distances those
  -- counts predict are out by 1.0 to 1.7. I validated a pose against a number I
  -- had myself proved unreliable. The mirror still holds: the trace cannot tell
  -- handedness (0.9056 as drawn against 0.9065 mirrored) and E18's twist arrow
  -- decides it.
  let blank = union(
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.887472, ny = -0.460862, nz = 0, d = 8.2933mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.130236, ny = -0.991483, nz = 0, d = 5.2374mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.579162, ny = 0.815212, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 7.4928mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.536069, ny = 0.844174, nz = 0, d = 6.3994mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.989169, ny = 0.146783, nz = 0, d = 3.1276mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.838285, ny = -0.545233, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 5.0980mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.294985, ny = 0.955502, nz = 0, d = 6.6311mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.838285, ny = 0.545233, nz = 0, d = 5.1015mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.683818, ny = -0.729652, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 5.8175mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.683818, ny = 0.729652, nz = 0, d = 5.8215mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.999743, ny = -0.022687, nz = 0, d = 2.1689mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.829418, ny = -0.558628, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 4.9588mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.358069, ny = 0.933695, nz = 0, d = 6.5980mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.829418, ny = 0.558628, nz = 0, d = 4.9627mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.747477, ny = -0.664287, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 5.3513mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.077287, ny = 0.997009, nz = 0, d = 6.6376mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.747477, ny = 0.664287, nz = 0, d = 5.3551mm),
      half_space(nx = 0.647358, ny = -0.762186, nz = 0, d = 0mm - 5.6587mm),
      box(x = 20mm, y = 20mm, z = datum.automatic_framework_thickness()),
    ),
    intersect(
      half_space(nx = -0.647358, ny = 0.762186, nz = 0, d = 5.6629mm),
      half_space(nx = -0.992885, ny = 0.119077, nz = 0, d = 2.9355mm),
